Package: rasch
Type: Package
Title: Models and Diagnostics for Rasch Measurement Theory
Version: 1.14.1
Authors@R: person("Josh", "McGrane", role = c("aut", "cre"),
    email = "drjoshmcgrane@gmail.com")
Description: Implements Rasch Measurement Theory for constructing and
    evaluating measurement scales. The package is organised around the two
    defining properties of Rasch measurement: sufficiency, whereby the
    relevant score contains the information required for person measurement,
    and invariance, whereby comparisons between persons and items are intended
    to remain stable across relevant samples and conditions. Models include
    the dichotomous Rasch model, partial credit and rating scale models,
    many-facet Rasch models, the extended frame of reference model, and
    Bradley-Terry-Luce models for paired comparisons. Tools are provided for
    item and person estimation, tests of fit, targeting, dimensionality, local
    response dependence, differential item functioning, threshold functioning,
    reliability, equating, and the investigation and resolution of violations
    of invariance. An interactive 'shiny' application provides a guided,
    point-and-click graphical interface for importing data, fitting models,
    reviewing diagnostics, and exporting results without requiring users to
    write R analysis code. Repeated-measures designs, simulation, graphical
    displays, and exportable reports are also supported. Estimation is by
    pairwise conditional maximum likelihood (Zwinderman, 1995
    <doi:10.1177/014662169501900406>; Andrich and Luo, 2003) with Godambe
    sandwich standard errors and composite-likelihood information criteria
    (Varin and Vidoni, 2005 <doi:10.1093/biomet/92.3.519>); person measures
    are weighted likelihood estimates (Warm, 1989 <doi:10.1007/BF02294627>);
    paired comparisons follow Bradley and Terry (1952)
    <doi:10.1093/biomet/39.3-4.324> as the conditional form of the
    dichotomous Rasch model (Andrich, 1978 <doi:10.1007/BF02293814>). The
    methods follow the measurement-theory framework described by Andrich and
    Marais (2019) <doi:10.1007/978-981-13-7496-8>.
URL: https://drjoshmcgrane.github.io/rasch/, https://github.com/drjoshmcgrane/rasch
